when was the last time anyone was on the moon,"December 14, 1972 (Apollo 17).","December 14, 1972","Authoritative mission records show Apollo 17 as the last crewed lunar landing. The final EVA concluded and the ascent from the lunar surface occurred on December 14, 1972 (UTC). Apollo missions 18–20 were canceled before any lunar landings, so there was no crewed lunar presence in 1974.","['14 December 1972 UTC', 'December 1972']"
who wrote he ain't heavy he's my brother lyrics,Bobby Scott and Bob Russell,Bobby Scott and Bob Russell,"The song ""He Ain't Heavy, He's My Brother"" was written in 1969 by composer Bobby Scott and lyricist Bob Russell. Although it became a hit for the Hollies (and later Neil Diamond), official songwriting credits and publishing records attribute it to Scott and Russell, not Paul McCartney. The British pop associations of the era can cause confusion, but the documented credits are clear.","['Bobby Scott', 'Bob Russell']"
how many seasons of the bastard executioner are there,1,1,"""The Bastard Executioner"" premiered on FX in September 2015 and was canceled in November 2015 after its initial run, with no renewal. Official statements from FX and creator Kurt Sutter confirmed there would be no second season. Any impression of two seasons likely comes from discussion spanning promotional cycles, but the series had only one season.","['one', 'one season']"
when did the eagles win last super bowl,"February 4, 2018.","February 4, 2018","The Eagles’ only Super Bowl championship is Super Bowl LII, played on February 4, 2018, when they defeated the New England Patriots. They returned to Super Bowl LVII in 2023 but lost to the Kansas City Chiefs, so 2023 is not a championship year. Therefore, their most recent Super Bowl win was on February 4, 2018.",['2017']
who won last year's ncaa women's basketball,South Carolina Gamecocks,South Carolina Gamecocks,"""Last year"" is ambiguous. To avoid guessing beyond my knowledge cutoff (October 2024), I use the most recent NCAA women's basketball champion I can verify: South Carolina won the 2024 national title (defeating Iowa). LSU won in 2023, not in 2024. Therefore, the correct answer under this interpretation is South Carolina Gamecocks.",['South Carolina']
when did the isle of wight become an island,"About 7,000 years ago, when post‑Ice Age sea levels flooded the Solent.","About 7,000 years ago (c. 6000-5000 BC)","Geological research indicates that post-glacial sea-level rise during the Holocene flooded the Solent river valley, isolating the Isle of Wight from the Hampshire coast roughly 7,000 years ago. This timing is supported by dated drowned forest remains and marine sediments in the Solent. Historical evidence further shows it was already an island in Roman times (known as Vectis), which rules out a post-Roman or 5th-century AD separation.",['During the last Ice Age']
love yourself by justin bieber is about who,"Selena Gomez (widely believed, never officially confirmed).",Selena Gomez,"The subject of the song has never been officially confirmed by Justin Bieber or the songwriters. However, widespread public and media consensus links it to Selena Gomez due to the timing of their on-and-off relationship and the pointed breakup lyrics, making her the commonly accepted answer.",['Rihanna']
who was the ruler of england in 1616,King James I,King James I,"Elizabeth I died in 1603, ending the Tudor dynasty. James VI of Scotland then became James I of England, beginning the Stuart dynasty, and he reigned from 1603 to 1625. Therefore, 1616 falls within his reign, making King James I the ruler of England in that year. Henry VIII reigned 1509–1547, which is decades earlier.",['James I']
what is the hot coffee mod in san andreas,"An unofficial mod for GTA: San Andreas that unlocks a hidden, disabled ""Hot Coffee"" minigame, allowing interactive sexual content with CJ’s girlfriends and triggering major controversy and ratings changes.","An unofficial mod/patch for GTA: San Andreas that unlocks a hidden sex minigame, exposing cut content and triggering the Hot Coffee controversy that led to ESRB rating changes and game recalls.","The user asked for the definition and emphasized that the term is commonly used in the context of the controversy, recalls, and ESRB response. I therefore defined Hot Coffee as the unofficial mod/patch that unlocks the hidden sexual minigame, highlighting its role in the controversy, matching the user’s framing.",['a normally inaccessible mini-game']
what is the maximum data rate for the 802.11a standard select one,54 Mbps,54 Mbps,"IEEE 802.11a defines OFDM PHY rates of 6, 9, 12, 18, 24, 36, 48, and 54 Mb/s; the maximum standard data rate is 54 Mb/s (64-QAM 3/4 on a 20 MHz channel). Practical throughput can be lower, but the question asks for the maximum data rate defined by the standard, which is 54 Mb/s.",['54\xa0Mbit/s']
which state is located in the centre of india,Madhya Pradesh,Madhya Pradesh,"Using the geographic midpoint of mainland India as the criterion, the Survey of India places this point near Karaundi in Jabalpur district, which lies in Madhya Pradesh. Therefore, the state located at the centre is Madhya Pradesh. Maharashtra covers a large central belt but does not contain the surveyed geographic centre.","['Chhattisgarh', 'Madhya Pradesh']"
who sang i ran all the way home,The Impalas,The Impalas,"The song commonly remembered as ""I Ran All the Way Home"" is actually titled ""Sorry (I Ran All the Way Home)"" and was recorded in 1959 by The Impalas, a Brooklyn doo-wop group on Cub Records. While its style resembles that of Dion and the Belmonts, they did not release this song; the hit version is by The Impalas.",['The Impalas']
where did the last name wallace come from,"A Scottish surname from Anglo-Norman French ""Waleis/Walensis"" meaning ""Welshman,"" originally denoting someone of Welsh or Brittonic (Strathclyde) origin; later popularized by William Wallace.","From Anglo-Norman French Waleis/Walensis meaning Welshman, a Norman byname for Britons (especially of Strathclyde) that became a Scottish hereditary surname and was later popularized by William Wallace.","Standard etymological authorities (e.g., the Oxford Dictionary of Family Names and Reaney & Wilson) derive Wallace from Anglo-Norman French ""Waleis/Walensis"" meaning ""Welshman""; in medieval Scotland it denoted Brittonic/Strathclyde people and became a hereditary surname. The proposed derivation from the personal name ""Walo"" is not the consensus and lacks comparable documentary support, whereas spellings like ""le Waleis"" are well attested in early records.",['a Scottish surname']
who was the actor that played ben stone on law and order,Michael Moriarty,Michael Moriarty,"Ben Stone was the original Executive Assistant District Attorney in seasons 1–4 of Law & Order, and official cast records credit Michael Moriarty with that role. Sam Waterston is often associated with the franchise, but he played Jack McCoy starting in season 5, not Ben Stone.",['Michael Moriarty']
who does the voice of nala in the lion king,Moira Kelly (1994 original); Beyoncé Knowles-Carter (2019 remake).,Moira Kelly (1994 original); Beyoncé Knowles-Carter (2019 remake).,"According to the films’ official credits, adult Nala’s speaking voice in the 1994 animated The Lion King is performed by Moira Kelly (with Niketa Calame voicing young Nala and Sally Dworsky/Laura Williams providing singing). In the 2019 remake, Nala is voiced by Beyoncé Knowles-Carter (with Shahadi Wright Joseph as young Nala). Jodi Benson is known for voicing Ariel in The Little Mermaid, not Nala, so the correct credited Nala voices are Moira Kelly (1994) and Beyoncé Knowles-Carter (2019).","['Niketa Calame', 'Sally Dworsky', 'Moira Kelly', 'Laura Williams']"
who plays gram on the young and the restless,Max Shippee,Max Shippee,"""Gram"" refers to Graham Bloodworth on The Young and the Restless, a character commonly called ""Graham."" He was portrayed by Max Shippee during the 2017 storyline. Hudson West is a child actor known for playing Jake Spencer on General Hospital, not a Y&R role. Therefore, the correct answer is Max Shippee.",['Max Shippee']
what is the oath that new citizens take,"I hereby declare, on oath, that I absolutely and entirely renounce and abjure all allegiance and fidelity to any foreign prince, potentate, state, or sovereignty, of whom or which I have heretofore been a subject or citizen; that I will support and defend the Constitution and laws of the United States of America against all enemies, foreign and domestic; that I will bear true faith and allegiance to the same; that I will bear arms on behalf of the United States when required by the law; that I will perform noncombatant service in the Armed Forces of the United States when required by the law; that I will perform work of national importance under civilian direction when required by the law; and that I take this obligation freely without any mental reservation or purpose of evasion; so help me God.","I hereby declare, on oath, that I absolutely and entirely renounce and abjure all allegiance and fidelity to any foreign prince, potentate, state, or sovereignty, of whom or which I have heretofore been a subject or citizen; that I will support and defend the Constitution and laws of the United States of America against all enemies, foreign and domestic; that I will bear true faith and allegiance to the same; that I will bear arms on behalf of the United States when required by the law; that I will perform noncombatant service in the Armed Forces of the United States when required by the law; that I will perform work of national importance under civilian direction when required by the law; and that I take this obligation freely without any mental reservation or purpose of evasion; so help me God.","The question asks for the oath new U.S. citizens take at naturalization. The official text, commonly called the Oath of Allegiance (also referred to as the Naturalization Pledge), is set by regulation and published by USCIS. I provided that standard wording verbatim; the closing phrase is traditionally included, though applicants may omit it by affirming.",['United States Oath of Allegiance']
who is under the mask of darth vader,Anakin Skywalker,Anakin Skywalker,"Canonical sources reveal that Darth Vader is Anakin Skywalker: he is unmasked as Anakin in Return of the Jedi (portrayed by Sebastian Shaw), and the prequel trilogy shows Anakin's transformation into Darth Vader. Bail Organa is Leia's adoptive father and not the person under Vader's mask.",['Anakin Skywalker']
who had a baby at 100 in the bible,Abraham,Abraham,"Genesis 21:5 explicitly states that Abraham was 100 years old when his son Isaac was born. Sarah, the mother, was 90, and Elizabeth's age is described only as advanced, not specified as 100. Therefore, the figure who had a child at age 100 in the Bible is Abraham.","['Sarah', 'Abraham']"
what age do you need to be to buy a bb gun,18+ in most places; laws vary by state/country and retailer—verify locally.,18,"Laws vary by jurisdiction and store policy, but BB guns are commonly restricted to adult purchase. In many places—including most U.S. states and the U.K.—you must be 18 to buy one, though minors may use them with supervision. Therefore, 18 is the most widely applied minimum age to purchase a BB gun.",['18']
ethiopia flight 961 crashes in to the sea,"On 23 Nov 1996, hijacked Ethiopian Airlines Flight 961 ditched in the Indian Ocean near the Comoros; 125 died, 50 survived.",23 November 1996,"Multiple authoritative sources, including aviation accident databases, contemporaneous news reports, and the official investigation, document that Ethiopian Airlines Flight 961 was hijacked and ditched near the Comoros on 1996-11-23. The 1995 date is a common misconception likely stemming from confusion with other mid-1990s regional incidents, but the verified crash date is late 1996.",['23 November 1996']
where was percy jackson and the olympians filmed,"Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada (primarily at Mammoth Studios in Burnaby and nearby locations).","Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada (primarily at Mammoth Studios in Burnaby and surrounding Metro Vancouver locations).","Industry reports and official production information for the Disney+ series ""Percy Jackson and the Olympians"" identify Metro Vancouver as the primary filming hub, with the show based at Mammoth Studios in Burnaby and using nearby Vancouver-area locations. Trade coverage and local media documented on-location shoots around Vancouver during principal photography. No credible production records place the series as Toronto-based.","['Vancouver', 'Mission, British Columbia']"
how many seasons of vampire diaries r there,8,8,"The Vampire Diaries aired on The CW from 2009 to 2017, concluding with its eighth and final season; official episode guides and reputable databases list a total of 8 seasons.","['eight', '8']"
how many episodes are there in dragon ball z,291,291,"Authoritative sources and the original Japanese broadcast list count 291 episodes for Dragon Ball Z (1989–1996). Some regional home-video releases repackage the show into fewer ""seasons"" with different episode groupings, which can total 198, but those are packaging counts, not the episode total. Therefore, the correct episode count is 291.","['291 episodes', '291']"
who plays auggie in the movie the wonder,Jacob Tremblay,Jacob Tremblay,"Auggie Pullman, the central child character in the 2017 film ""Wonder"" (based on R.J. Palacio's novel), is portrayed by Jacob Tremblay. Owen Wilson appears in the same film as Auggie's father, Nate Pullman, not as Auggie.",['Jacob Tremblay']
when was the first australian prime minister elected,1 January 1901,29-30 March 1901,"Australia's prime minister is not directly elected; Edmund Barton was appointed and sworn in on 1 January 1901. If one ties 'elected' to the first national vote that established parliamentary support for the government, the first federal election was held on 29-30 March 1901 (not 1903). Therefore, the election corresponding to the first prime minister's selection occurred on 29-30 March 1901.","['Sir Edmund Barton', '1901']"
who plays matthew on anne with an e,R. H. Thomson,R. H. Thomson,"The role of Matthew Cuthbert in the CBC/Netflix series ""Anne with an E"" is credited to R. H. Thomson. Although the user suggests Colm Feore, official cast lists and on-screen credits confirm that R. H. Thomson portrays Matthew in this series.",['R. H. Thomson']
who is the girl in green day 21 guns,Lisa Stelly.,Lisa Stelly,"The question asks for the woman in Green Day's official 2009 ""21 Guns"" music video. The credited female lead in that video is Lisa Stelly. Confusion often stems from separate collaborations and promotions involving the ""American Idiot"" cast and discussions linking Evan Rachel Wood to the franchise, but she does not appear in the official ""21 Guns"" video.",['Lisa Stelly']
who plays joker in batman the dark knight,Heath Ledger,Heath Ledger,"The question specifies ""The Dark Knight"" (2008), directed by Christopher Nolan, in which Heath Ledger played the Joker. Jack Nicholson portrayed the Joker in Tim Burton's 1989 film ""Batman,"" a different movie in the franchise. Therefore, the correct actor for the Joker in ""The Dark Knight"" is Heath Ledger.",['Ledger']
when was the south asian association for regional co-operation (saarc) formed,8 December 1985,8 December 1985,SAARC was officially formed when its Charter was signed by the heads of state/government at the first SAARC Summit in Dhaka on 8 December 1985. The 1983 foreign ministers' declaration laid the groundwork but did not constitute formal formation.,"['December 1985', '8 December 1985']"
when was the first hunger games book published,"September 14, 2008","September 14, 2008","Authoritative records from the publisher (Scholastic Press) and bibliographic databases (e.g., Library of Congress) list the U.S. first-edition hardcover publication date as September 14, 2008. Contemporary reviews and bestseller appearances also begin in September 2008. Recollections of an earlier date likely refer to the 2007 deal announcements or early advance reader copies, not the retail publication.","['September 14, 2008', '2008']"
who did bette midler portray in the rose,"Mary Rose Foster (""The Rose"").",Mary Rose Foster,"Authoritative sources and the film's credits identify Bette Midler's character as Mary Rose Foster, a rock singer nicknamed ""The Rose."" There is no credited character named Rose Hearn; that name appears to be a misconception.",['Mary Rose Foster']
who is the lead singer of depeche mode,Dave Gahan,Dave Gahan,"Depeche Mode’s official lineup and widely accepted sources identify Dave Gahan as the band’s lead vocalist/frontman. Martin Gore is the principal songwriter and sings lead on some tracks, but the primary lead singer role is held by Gahan.",['David Gahan']
where did the butchers in the slaughterhouse cases live,"New Orleans, Louisiana.","New Orleans, Louisiana","The plaintiffs were the Butchers' Benevolent Association of New Orleans, and the challenged statute created a slaughterhouse monopoly serving New Orleans; the case caption and contemporary accounts identify them as New Orleans butchers, indicating they lived in New Orleans, Louisiana.",['New Orleans']
who developed the concept of total quality management,W. Edwards Deming,W. Edwards Deming,"While several pioneers shaped quality management, most standard references and management texts credit W. Edwards Deming with developing Total Quality Management as a holistic, organization-wide philosophy; the U.S. Navy popularized the term in the 1980s drawing on Deming's principles. Joseph M. Juran and Armand V. Feigenbaum made major contributions (e.g., Juran's quality trilogy and Feigenbaum's total quality control), but they are not most commonly cited as the developers of TQM.",['W. Edwards Deming']
who wrote cant get you out of my head lyrics,Cathy Dennis and Rob Davis.,Cathy Dennis and Rob Davis,"Authoritative credits for Kylie Minogue's 2001 single list Cathy Dennis and Rob Davis as the songwriters; although Stock Aitken Waterman wrote many of Minogue's late-1980s hits, they did not write 'Can't Get You Out of My Head'.","['Cathy Dennis and Rob Davis', 'Rob Davis', 'Cathy Dennis']"
where does no game no life anime end,"At the end of light novel volume 3 (episode 12), after the Eastern Federation/warbeast arc; continue from volume 4.",At the end of light novel volume 3 (episode 12).,"The 12-episode TV series adapts light novel volumes 1–3 and finishes with the Eastern Federation/warbeast arc in episode 12. The subsequent movie adapts the distant prequel (volume 6), not the mainline continuation, so the TV anime’s story stops at the end of volume 3; volumes 4–5 were not covered.","['the Elkia Federation', 'the sixth volume']"
when did taylor swift's first album release,"October 24, 2006","October 24, 2006","Authoritative release records and contemporary press from Big Machine Records confirm that Taylor Swift's self-titled debut album was released on October 24, 2006. The confusion with 2007 stems from the staggered rollout: the lead single ""Tim McGraw"" arrived in June 2006, while subsequent singles (e.g., ""Teardrops on My Guitar"") peaked and expanded radio/TV exposure through 2007, making it seem like the album arrived later. Despite that promotional timeline, the official album release date remains October 24, 2006.","['October 24, 2006', '2005']"
what are the three fifty shades of grey books,"Fifty Shades of Grey, Fifty Shades Darker, and Fifty Shades Freed.","Grey: Fifty Shades of Grey as Told by Christian, Darker: Fifty Shades Darker as Told by Christian, Freed: Fifty Shades Freed as Told by Christian","The user clarified they were referring to the companion trilogy that retells the original series from Christian Grey's perspective. That trilogy consists of three books released in order: Grey: Fifty Shades of Grey as Told by Christian (2015), Darker: Fifty Shades Darker as Told by Christian (2017), and Freed: Fifty Shades Freed as Told by Christian (2021). Therefore, those are the three titles requested.","['Fifty Shades of Grey', 'Fifty Shades Darker', 'Fifty Shades Freed']"
who has the power (judicial) to make decisions in courts of law,Judges.,Judges,"""Judicial power"" is the authority to interpret and apply the law in courts, and it is vested in the judiciary; judges exercise this power by making legal rulings, managing proceedings, and entering judgments. While juries may determine facts and deliver verdicts in some cases, they do not hold the judicial power itself.",['judges']
what are the ranks in the us navy,"Enlisted (E-1 to E-9): Seaman Recruit, Seaman Apprentice, Seaman, Petty Officer Third Class, Petty Officer Second Class, Petty Officer First Class, Chief Petty Officer, Senior Chief Petty Officer, Master Chief Petty Officer (incl. Command/Fleet/Force Master Chief and the Master Chief Petty Officer of the Navy). Warrant (W-1 to W-5): Warrant Officer 1; Chief Warrant Officer 2, 3, 4, 5. Commissioned (O-1 to O-10): Ensign; Lieutenant (Junior Grade); Lieutenant; Lieutenant Commander; Commander; Captain; Rear Admiral (Lower Half); Rear Admiral (Upper Half); Vice Admiral; Admiral. Wartime only: Fleet Admiral (five-star).",Enlisted (E-1 to E-9): Seaman Recruit; Seaman Apprentice; Seaman; Petty Officer Third Class; Petty Officer Second Class; Petty Officer First Class; Chief Petty Officer; Senior Chief Petty Officer; Master Chief Petty Officer (including Command/Fleet/Force Master Chief and the Master Chief Petty Officer of the Navy). Warrant (W-1 to W-5): Warrant Officer 1; Chief Warrant Officer 2; Chief Warrant Officer 3; Chief Warrant Officer 4; Chief Warrant Officer 5. Commissioned (O-1 to O-10): Ensign; Lieutenant (Junior Grade); Lieutenant; Lieutenant Commander; Commander; Captain; Rear Admiral (Lower Half); Rear Admiral (Upper Half); Vice Admiral; Admiral. Wartime only: Fleet Admiral.,"I used the official U.S. Department of Defense/Navy paygrade structure. The enlisted ranks run from E-1 through E-9, with E-9 (Master Chief Petty Officer) as the highest enlisted rank; there is no E-10. Titles such as Command/Fleet/Force Master Chief and the Master Chief Petty Officer of the Navy are E-9 billets/titles, not higher paygrades. Warrant officer grades are W-1 through W-5, and commissioned officer grades are O-1 through O-10, with Fleet Admiral as a five-star wartime-only grade.","['E-8s senior chief petty officer', 'E-9s master chief petty officer']"
i was a great islamic scholar and mathematician who died in 1131 ce,Omar Khayyam,Omar Khayyam,"The clue points to a famed Islamic scholar and mathematician who died in 1131 CE. Omar Khayyam, a Persian polymath known for major contributions to algebra and astronomy, is widely recorded to have died in 1131 CE. Al-Ghazali, while a towering scholar, died in 1111 CE, so he does not match the specified year.",['Omar Khayyam']
who is the coach for the ottawa senators,Travis Green,Travis Green,"D.J. Smith served as head coach from 2019 until he was fired in December 2023, after which Jacques Martin was interim. In May 2024, the Senators hired Travis Green as head coach. Therefore, the correct current coach is Travis Green.",['Guy Boucher']
last episode of what happens to my family,"Episode 53 (aired February 15, 2015).",53,"Official episode counts list 'What Happens to My Family?' with a total of 53 episodes due to an extension beyond the typical weekend-drama run; the finale aired on February 15, 2015, making Episode 53 the last episode.",['53']
when is the last time the vikings were in the nfc championship,"January 21, 2018 (2017 season), vs. the Eagles.","January 21, 2018","The Vikings most recently reached the NFC Championship Game in the 2017 season after defeating the Saints in the Divisional Round and then played the Eagles on January 21, 2018. This is later than their 2009 season appearance, so 2018 is the correct and most recent date.","['1976', '2017/18']"
what are the colors of the netherlands flag,"Red, white, and blue.","Red, white, and blue","The Netherlands' national flag is officially a horizontal tricolor of red (top), white (middle), and blue (bottom), as specified by Dutch law and standard vexillological references. Orange is used for royal symbolism and appears in historical variants, but it is not part of the current flag.","['blue', 'white', 'red']"
where is lord's prayer found in bible,Matthew 6:9–13 and Luke 11:2–4.,Matthew 6:9-13; Luke 11:2-4,"The question asks where the Lord's Prayer is found in the Bible. It appears in two places: Matthew 6:9-13 (within the Sermon on the Mount) and a shorter, parallel version in Luke 11:2-4. Therefore, the correct answer includes both references.",['in the Gospel of Luke']
when was the last time oklahoma won a national championship in football,2000,2000,"Oklahoma's officially recognized football national championships are 1950, 1955, 1956, 1974, 1975, 1985, and 2000. In the 2004 season, Oklahoma lost the BCS title game to USC; although USC's title was later vacated, it was not reassigned to Oklahoma. Therefore, the Sooners' most recent national championship is from the 2000 season.","['2003', '2000']"
location of the ten commandments in the bible,Exodus 20:1-17 and Deuteronomy 5:6-21.,Exodus 20:1-17; Deuteronomy 5:6-21,The Ten Commandments are explicitly listed twice in the Bible: first when given at Mount Sinai (Exodus 20:1-17) and then when Moses reiterates them to Israel (Deuteronomy 5:6-21). Leviticus contains extensive laws and rituals but does not present the Decalogue itself; therefore the correct locations are Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5.,"['Exodus', 'Deuteronomy']"
where did the allies go after north africa,"Sicily, then mainland Italy.","Sicily, then mainland Italy.","The North African campaign concluded in May 1943. The Allies immediately launched Operation Husky, invading Sicily in July 1943, and then moved onto mainland Italy with landings in September 1943. The invasion of southern France (Operation Dragoon) occurred later in August 1944, so it was not the direct follow-up to North Africa.","['Italy', 'the Italian Campaign']"
what is the meaning of veronica in english,"Bearer of victory; also interpreted as ""true image.""",true image,"The name Veronica has two noted derivations: from Greek Berenike (bearer of victory) and from the Latin devotional phrase vera icon (true image). In English-name explanations, especially within Christian tradition, the Latin-based folk etymology became the standard gloss. Therefore, the most common English meaning given for Veronica is true image.",['she who brings victory']
what is the name of the main artery which takes blood from the heart to the body,Aorta,Aorta,"The main systemic artery is the aorta, which carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the entire body. In contrast, the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs only, not to the body’s systemic circulation.","['The aorta', 'aorta']"
who played solomon in little house on the prairie,Todd Bridges,Todd Bridges,"Solomon (Solomon Henry) appears in the Season 3 episode ""The Wisdom of Solomon"" of Little House on the Prairie, and the episode credits list Todd Bridges as the actor. Shavar Ross did not portray Solomon on the series.",['Todd Bridges']
who does eric end up with in that 70s show,Donna Pinciotti,Donna Pinciotti,"In the series finale of That '70s Show, Eric returns and he and Donna reconcile and kiss at midnight on New Year's Eve 1979, confirming they end up together; Jackie ends up with Fez, not Eric.",['Donna']
an object that moves around an external axis is said to be,revolving,revolving,"In standard mechanics, motion about an internal axis is called ""rotation"" (spin), while motion about an external axis is called ""revolution""; therefore an object moving around an external axis is ""revolving."" A common example is that Earth rotates on its axis but revolves around the Sun.","['revolution or orbital revolution', 'orbit']"
architectural elements forming rib vaults eg wells cathedral,"Diagonal, transverse, and ridge ribs with thin webbing; ribs spring from clustered shafts/compound piers and meet in bosses.","Diagonal (ogive) ribs, transverse and longitudinal (ridge/wall) ribs, with thin webbing; ribs spring from clustered shafts/compound piers and meet in bosses; later English work adds tiercerons and liernes.","The question asks for the architectural elements that form rib vaults, exemplified by Wells Cathedral. In a rib vault, the structural and visual framework is provided by the ribs themselves: diagonal (ogive) ribs across the bay, transverse ribs between bays, and longitudinal ridge/wall ribs along the nave; these define the vault’s geometry. The spaces between are filled with thin webbing, and the ribs converge at bosses. These ribs spring from clustered shafts or compound piers. While medieval designers often laid out rib patterns along the lines of intersecting groins, a groin vault is not itself the forming element of a rib vault; rather, the ribs articulate and carry the system. In later English Gothic, additional ribs such as tiercerons and liernes appear, including at Wells in later phases.","['an armature of piped masonry', 'barrel vaults', 'two to three barrel vaults']"
who sang the song good morning good morning,The Beatles,John Lennon,"The song ""Good Morning Good Morning"" appears on the Beatles' 1967 album Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band, but the lead vocals on the track are performed by John Lennon; therefore, the singer is John Lennon.","['Gene Kelly', ""Donald O'Connor"", 'Judy Garland', 'Debbie Reynolds', 'Mickey Rooney']"
who wrote papa got a brand new bag,James Brown,James Brown,"The song ""Papa's Got a Brand New Bag"" is officially credited to James Brown. Original 1965 King Records releases and music rights databases list James Brown as the sole songwriter; Otis Redding performed in a similar era and style but is not credited as the writer of this song.",['James Brown']
where does a roadrunner live in the desert,On the ground in open desert scrub—among cacti and low shrubs—in the southwestern U.S. and northern Mexico.,On open desert flats with broad sandy terrain and scattered cacti and scrub.,"I used your clarification together with common habitat descriptions for roadrunners. They depend on visibility and speed to hunt, so they occupy open, sparsely vegetated desert flats rather than dense cover. This leads to the concise answer: ""open desert flats with broad sandy terrain and scattered cacti and scrub.""","['mountainous shrubland', 'arid lowland']"
who wrote it's a long long way to pasadena,"Grant Clarke, Edgar Leslie, and Harry Warren.","Grant Clarke, Edgar Leslie, and Harry Warren.","""It's a Long, Long Way to Pasadena"" is a variant title/lyric line for the 1923 song ""Home in Pasadena,"" which credits lyrics to Grant Clarke and Edgar Leslie and music to Harry Warren. Later 1960s Australian recordings may be associated with the Easybeats circle, but the original songwriting credit remains with Clarke, Leslie, and Warren, not Stevie Wright.","['John Young', 'Harry Vanda', 'David Hemmings', 'George Young']"
what's the dog's name on tom and jerry,Spike,Spike,"In Tom and Jerry, the recurring bulldog character is officially named ""Spike"" (often seen with his pup, Tyke). ""Butch"" is the name of the black alley cat who is Tom's rival, not the bulldog.",['Spike']
who are nominated for president of india 2017,Ram Nath Kovind and Meira Kumar.,Ram Nath Kovind and Meira Kumar,"The official nominees for the 2017 Indian presidential election were Ram Nath Kovind (NDA) and Meira Kumar (UPA). They were formally announced by their alliances in June 2017 and filed nominations with the Election Commission. Although figures like L. K. Advani and Hamid Ansari were speculated in media discussions, they were not the final nominees.","['Meira Kumar', 'Ram Nath Kovind']"
where does the path train stop in newark,Newark Penn Station,Newark Penn Station,"PATH’s Newark–World Trade Center line terminates at Newark Penn Station, which is the only PATH stop within the city of Newark. The Newark Liberty International Airport rail station is served by NJ Transit and Amtrak via the AirTrain, not by PATH; a PATH extension to the airport has been proposed but is not open. Therefore, the PATH train’s stop in Newark is Newark Penn Station.",['Newark Penn Station']
where does route 66 start on the west coast,"Santa Monica, California, near the Santa Monica Pier (historic terminus at Lincoln Blvd & Olympic Blvd).","Santa Monica, California (Lincoln Blvd & Olympic Blvd)","Route 66’s officially designated western terminus was extended in 1936 from downtown Los Angeles to Santa Monica, with the endpoint at Lincoln Boulevard and Olympic Boulevard. Therefore, when starting the route on the West Coast, the recognized starting point is Santa Monica; references to downtown Los Angeles reflect the earlier 1926-1936 terminus, not the coastal start.",['in Santa Monica']
who is the first wife on sister wives,Meri Brown,Meri Brown,"Authoritative accounts of the show and the family state that Meri Brown is Kody Browns first wife; they legally married in 1990, before his spiritual marriages to Janelle (1993) and Christine (1994). Confusion can arise from household dynamics, but chronologically and legally, the first wife is Meri.",['Meri']
when did houston go to the american league,2013,2013,"The Houston Astros move to the American League took effect at the start of the 2013 MLB season, when they shifted from the National League Central to the American League West as part of MLBs realignment to balance 15 teams per league; they still played in the National League in 2012.",['2013']
when did they stop cigarette advertising on television,"January 2, 1971 (U.S.).","January 2, 1971","In the U.S., the Public Health Cigarette Smoking Act of 1970 banned cigarette advertising on television and radio effective immediately after 11:59 p.m. on January 1, 1971. The last TV cigarette ads aired that night, so the first day without such advertising—and thus the date they effectively stopped—was January 2, 1971.","['January 2, 1971', '1970']"
coldplay song i will try to fix you,Fix You,Fix You,"The phrase ""I will try to fix you"" is a direct lyric from Coldplay's song ""Fix You"" (notably in the chorus: ""Lights will guide you home... and I will try to fix you""). It does not appear in ""The Scientist,"" so the correct song title is ""Fix You.""","['""Fix You""']"
points on a sphere or angles in a circle are measured in units called,degrees,degrees,"Angles around a circle are measured in degrees (360 degrees for a full circle), and positions on a sphere—such as latitude and longitude—are also expressed in degrees; therefore, the unit asked for is degrees.",['radians']
the oligodynamic effect is a phenomenon that describes,"The antimicrobial action of minute amounts of certain heavy metals (e.g., silver, copper) against microorganisms.","The antimicrobial action of minute amounts of certain heavy metals (e.g., silver, copper) against microorganisms.","Standard scientific usage defines the ""oligodynamic effect"" as the marked antimicrobial/biocidal action exerted by trace amounts of certain metals, notably silver and copper, first described by Naegeli (1893). The alternative description given—catalytic promotion of colloid coagulation by ions—corresponds to electrolyte-induced coagulation in colloid chemistry (e.g., the Hardy-Schulze rule), which is a different phenomenon. Therefore, the correct definition is the antimicrobial action of minute quantities of specific heavy metals against microorganisms.",['a biocidal effect of metals']
where does the white witch live in narnia,In her castle in the far north of Narnia.,In her castle in the far north of Narnia.,"In The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe, the White Witch (Jadis) is shown residing in her own northern castle—often referred to as the Witch's House—where she keeps her stone-statue victims. Cair Paravel is the rightful Narnian royal seat and is not her residence; she rules from her northern stronghold during the long winter.",['her castle']
how many games in a row have the uconn women's basketball team won,111,111,"The question is interpreted as asking for the program’s longest winning streak. UConn’s women’s basketball team set the NCAA record with 111 consecutive victories from November 2014 until their loss to Mississippi State on March 31, 2017. The commonly cited figure of 102 was a milestone reached during that run, but the streak ultimately extended to 111 before ending.","['111 straight wins', '111', '90']"
is there a name for the at symbol,"Yes—it's called the at sign (also ""commercial at"").",at sign (commercial at),"I consulted standard naming conventions and terminology: the Unicode standard names the character U+0040 as ""COMMERCIAL AT,"" and in contemporary English usage it is commonly called the ""at sign."" While historical bookkeeping sometimes referred to it as a mercantile symbol, the prevailing modern names are ""at sign"" and ""commercial at.""","['commercial at', 'at symbol', 'at sign']"
who sings the theme song for the tv show cops,Inner Circle,Inner Circle,"The TV show ""Cops"" uses the song ""Bad Boys"" as its theme. ""Bad Boys"" is performed by the Jamaican reggae group Inner Circle, not Gloria Estefan. Therefore, the singer/performer of the ""Cops"" theme is Inner Circle.","['Inner Circle', 'Jamaican reggae band Inner Circle']"
when was corporal punishment banned in south africa,Schools: 1996 (in force 1997); judicial: 1995 (fully abolished by 1997 Act); home: 2019.,Schools: 1996 (South African Schools Act; in force 1 Jan 1997); Judicial: 1997 (Abolition of Corporal Punishment Act; following a 1995 ruling for juveniles); Home: 2019 (Constitutional Court ended the 'reasonable chastisement' defense).,"The post-apartheid Constitution (1996) anchored rights, but the bans were implemented through specific laws and court rulings. In schools, Section 10 of the South African Schools Act 84 of 1996 prohibited corporal punishment (commencing 1 Jan 1997). Judicial corporal punishment was fully abolished by the Abolition of Corporal Punishment Act 33 of 1997, building on a 1995 Constitutional Court decision that had already outlawed juvenile whipping. In 2019, the Constitutional Court struck down the common-law 'reasonable chastisement' defense, effectively banning corporal punishment in the home. Because the question did not specify the setting, the answer lists the relevant dates for schools, courts, and the home.",['1997']
who plays captain phasma in star wars the force awakens,Gwendoline Christie,Gwendoline Christie,"Authoritative sources and the official film credits for Star Wars The Force Awakens identify Gwendoline Christie as the actor who portrays Captain Phasma, not Katee Sackhoff.",['Gwendoline Christie']
